<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n36650" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">plēnĭtūdo</span></b> ,
			ĭnis, f.
		id., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a
				being full</i>, <i>fulness</i>, <i>plenitude</i> (generally post-Aug.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Completeness</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">plenitudo syllabarum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Auct. Her. 4, 20, 28</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Fulness</i>, <i>abundance</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">terrae (i.e. frugum),</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg. Deut. 33, 16 al.</span></i>
			—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>III.</b>
			<i>Thickness</i>, <i>stoutness</i>, e.
			g. of a rod, of a man (post-Aug.), <i><span class="en">Col. 4, 30,
				4</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Plin. 11, 37, 87, §
		216</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>